{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Let the day perish</b> — He longed that the day on which he was born first perish — if he was born by day, and if he was born by night, that the night perish, for the harbinger said: <i>הורה גבר</i> (a male is born), and similarly: <i>ויאמר ליוסף</i> (and said to Joseph)." "<b>And born</b> — like <i>יולד</i> (is born), and similarly: <i>ותהר את מרים</i> (and she conceived Miriam), for the day on which he was born will be a day of darkness. The correct interpretation is that he longed that it be a day of darkness, to the point that it not be a day at all, and this is the meaning of <b>let the day perish</b>. And night and day are toward the earth from the direction of the sun, because the earth is in the middle of the spheres and the host above, and there is no darkness for them at all. Also the dark spots of the moon are from the direction of the earth, for below there is no light except from above, and this is the matter of <i>אל ידרשהו אלוה ממעל</i> (let him not seek El from above), for the earth is darkness."], :book :job, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The two Hebrew citations appear truncated or abbreviated in the source; they are rendered as they stand. The verse reference for 'a male is born' is Jeremiah 20:15; 'and said to Joseph' is unclear in this context and may be corrupt or elliptical.", 1 "The citation 'ותהר את מרים' does not correspond to any known biblical verse and may be corrupt or a paraphrastic reference. The citation 'אל ידרשהו אלוה ממעל' likewise does not match any standard verse and may reflect a variant tradition or an elliptical allusion."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Job -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/eyov/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:01:40.161442442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}